1
00:00:00,065 --> 00:00:02,265
Here's an experiment you
can do with your lunch.

2
00:00:02,485 --> 00:00:03,580
Take a plain cracker,

3
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:04,635
and keep chewing.

4
00:00:04,770 --> 00:00:05,755
Don't swallow.

5
00:00:05,965 --> 00:00:07,130
Give it a minute or so…

6
00:00:07,150 --> 00:00:08,815
and it starts to taste sweet.

7
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:10,570
But you didn't add any sugar.

8
00:00:10,660 --> 00:00:12,960
So where's that
sweetness coming from?

9
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:14,865
A molecule in your saliva,

10
00:00:14,885 --> 00:00:17,865
doing something that would
otherwise take millions of years.

11
00:00:20,741 --> 00:00:22,846
Crackers are packed with starch.

12
00:00:22,866 --> 00:00:25,250
Zoom right in, and starch is a chain:

13
00:00:25,395 --> 00:00:26,910
hundreds, even thousands,

14
00:00:26,930 --> 00:00:28,401
of glucose molecules,

15
00:00:28,421 --> 00:00:29,755
linked end to end.

16
00:00:30,191 --> 00:00:31,466
Glucose is a sugar,

17
00:00:31,486 --> 00:00:33,261
but locked up in a chain like this,

18
00:00:33,291 --> 00:00:34,620
it doesn't taste sweet.

19
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:36,485
And those links are tough.

20
00:00:36,505 --> 00:00:37,881
Left alone in water,

21
00:00:37,901 --> 00:00:40,651
a single link would take
millions of years to break.

22
00:00:40,826 --> 00:00:42,161
Enter amylase:

23
00:00:42,181 --> 00:00:43,891
an enzyme in your saliva.

24
00:00:44,151 --> 00:00:46,436
It snips starch into smaller sugars,

25
00:00:46,456 --> 00:00:49,971
like maltose, which is just two
glucose units joined together.

26
00:00:50,246 --> 00:00:52,201
And maltose does taste sweet.

27
00:00:52,481 --> 00:00:53,896
That's what an enzyme is:

28
00:00:53,916 --> 00:00:55,711
a biological catalyst.

29
00:00:55,896 --> 00:00:58,546
It speeds up a reaction
without being used up.

30
00:00:58,566 --> 00:01:01,386
And nearly every reaction
in your body has one.

31
00:01:01,406 --> 00:01:06,071
Without enzymes, the chemistry that
keeps you alive would be far too slow.

32
00:01:06,091 --> 00:01:08,321
Nearly all enzymes are proteins,

33
00:01:08,386 --> 00:01:12,326
long chains of amino acids
folded into a precise shape.

34
00:01:12,346 --> 00:01:13,996
And that shape is everything.

35
00:01:14,171 --> 00:01:17,271
Every enzyme has a small
pocket called the active site.

36
00:01:17,521 --> 00:01:18,921
The molecule it works on,

37
00:01:18,941 --> 00:01:21,050
the substrate, fits inside,

38
00:01:21,111 --> 00:01:23,556
forming an enzyme–substrate complex.

39
00:01:23,861 --> 00:01:27,061
Held there, a bond becomes
much easier to break.

40
00:01:27,081 --> 00:01:29,051
Snap. The products leave,

41
00:01:29,071 --> 00:01:31,776
and the active site is
free for the next one.

42
00:01:31,796 --> 00:01:34,831
The active site's shape is
complementary to the substrate:

43
00:01:35,081 --> 00:01:37,651
they fit together like
a lock and a key.

44
00:01:37,671 --> 00:01:40,280
That's why enzymes are so specific.

45
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:42,700
Amylase can't even
break down cellulose,

46
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:45,586
in paper, though that's
a chain of glucose too.

47
00:01:45,856 --> 00:01:47,666
Its links are joined differently,

48
00:01:47,686 --> 00:01:49,085
so it just doesn't fit.

49
00:01:49,258 --> 00:01:51,228
Lock and key is a brilliant model,

50
00:01:51,248 --> 00:01:52,868
but it is a simplification.

51
00:01:53,098 --> 00:01:55,053
Real enzymes aren't rigid.

52
00:01:55,308 --> 00:01:56,623
As the substrate binds,

53
00:01:56,643 --> 00:01:58,858
the active site moulds
itself around it.

54
00:01:59,143 --> 00:02:01,948
That more accurate picture
is called induced fit.

55
00:02:02,238 --> 00:02:05,178
For GCSE and IGCSE,

56
00:02:05,248 --> 00:02:07,663
lock and key is the model to use.

57
00:02:07,683 --> 00:02:08,713
Just remember:

58
00:02:08,788 --> 00:02:10,458
it's a model, not a photograph.

59
00:02:10,845 --> 00:02:12,595
Which brings us to myth number one:

60
00:02:12,765 --> 00:02:14,505
that enzymes get used up.

61
00:02:14,525 --> 00:02:15,565
They don't.

62
00:02:15,585 --> 00:02:16,950
After each reaction,

63
00:02:16,970 --> 00:02:18,790
the enzyme is unchanged,

64
00:02:18,815 --> 00:02:19,830
ready to go again,

65
00:02:19,850 --> 00:02:20,865
and again.

66
00:02:20,885 --> 00:02:23,030
And some are astonishingly fast.

67
00:02:23,255 --> 00:02:24,830
One molecule of catalase,

68
00:02:24,850 --> 00:02:29,195
from your liver, can break down millions
of molecules of hydrogen peroxide

69
00:02:29,215 --> 00:02:30,015
a second.

70
00:02:30,105 --> 00:02:31,835
Enzymes don't last forever:

71
00:02:31,925 --> 00:02:33,775
your cells replace old ones.

72
00:02:33,990 --> 00:02:36,380
But the reaction itself
never uses them up.

73
00:02:36,759 --> 00:02:37,914
Now, temperature.

74
00:02:38,044 --> 00:02:40,304
Molecules are always jiggling around.

75
00:02:40,499 --> 00:02:42,634
Heat them up, and they move faster.

76
00:02:42,654 --> 00:02:45,639
Enzymes and substrates bump
into each other more often,

77
00:02:45,659 --> 00:02:47,284
and with more energy.

78
00:02:47,304 --> 00:02:50,874
More successful collisions means
more reactions every second.

79
00:02:51,064 --> 00:02:53,499
Up to a point, warmer means faster.

80
00:02:53,814 --> 00:02:55,379
For many enzymes in your body,

81
00:02:55,399 --> 00:02:56,859
that peak, the optimum,

82
00:02:56,879 --> 00:02:58,609
is around 37 degrees:

83
00:02:58,714 --> 00:02:59,809
body temperature.

84
00:03:00,097 --> 00:03:01,832
So what if we keep
turning up the heat?

85
00:03:01,912 --> 00:03:02,937
Fifty degrees.

86
00:03:03,052 --> 00:03:04,697
Sixty. Seventy.

87
00:03:04,757 --> 00:03:06,832
Does it just keep getting faster?

88
00:03:06,852 --> 00:03:09,992
Pause the video, and
make your prediction.

89
00:03:13,062 --> 00:03:14,562
Here's what actually happens.

90
00:03:14,737 --> 00:03:16,932
The rate crashes, and
it doesn't recover.

91
00:03:17,122 --> 00:03:20,737
An enzyme's shape is held
together by lots of weak bonds.

92
00:03:20,757 --> 00:03:25,167
Too much heat, and it vibrates so
violently that those bonds break.

93
00:03:25,187 --> 00:03:26,552
The chain unravels,

94
00:03:26,572 --> 00:03:28,487
the active site loses its shape,

95
00:03:28,637 --> 00:03:30,687
and the substrate no longer fits.

96
00:03:30,922 --> 00:03:32,942
The enzyme has been denatured.

97
00:03:32,962 --> 00:03:36,857
It's the same kind of change that
turns egg white solid in a hot pan,

98
00:03:37,087 --> 00:03:38,427
and, like a cooked egg,

99
00:03:38,557 --> 00:03:40,742
cooling it down usually
doesn't undo it.

100
00:03:40,892 --> 00:03:41,957
Cold is different:

101
00:03:41,977 --> 00:03:43,742
it just slows enzymes down.

102
00:03:43,932 --> 00:03:45,592
Warm them up, and they recover.

103
00:03:45,891 --> 00:03:47,346
And that's myth number two:

104
00:03:47,416 --> 00:03:49,131
that heat "kills" enzymes.

105
00:03:49,356 --> 00:03:51,296
Enzymes were never alive.

106
00:03:51,316 --> 00:03:53,051
An enzyme is a molecule,

107
00:03:53,071 --> 00:03:54,086
a very clever one,

108
00:03:54,106 --> 00:03:55,246
but it can't die.

109
00:03:55,486 --> 00:03:56,646
It's denatured:

110
00:03:56,666 --> 00:03:58,446
its active site changes shape,

111
00:03:58,466 --> 00:04:00,791
so the substrate no longer fits.

112
00:04:00,811 --> 00:04:03,656
In an exam, that's the
answer that gets the marks.

113
00:04:03,961 --> 00:04:05,331
pH can do this too.

114
00:04:05,526 --> 00:04:07,141
Too acidic, or too alkaline,

115
00:04:07,161 --> 00:04:10,131
and the bonds holding the active
site in shape are disrupted.

116
00:04:10,421 --> 00:04:12,876
Go far enough, and the
enzyme is denatured.

117
00:04:12,896 --> 00:04:15,461
So every enzyme has an optimum pH.

118
00:04:15,706 --> 00:04:16,826
Amylase, in your mouth,

119
00:04:16,846 --> 00:04:18,341
works best close to neutral,

120
00:04:18,361 --> 00:04:19,896
around pH 7.

121
00:04:19,916 --> 00:04:21,626
Your stomach is strongly acidic,

122
00:04:21,646 --> 00:04:23,211
often around pH 2,

123
00:04:23,286 --> 00:04:26,106
and pepsin, the enzyme that
digests protein there,

124
00:04:26,126 --> 00:04:28,046
works best right around that.

125
00:04:28,066 --> 00:04:29,051
Different enzymes,

126
00:04:29,071 --> 00:04:30,551
tuned to different places.

127
00:04:30,912 --> 00:04:32,447
So, back to that cracker.

128
00:04:32,672 --> 00:04:35,162
As you chew, amylase
meets the starch.

129
00:04:35,427 --> 00:04:37,187
The chains fit its active site,

130
00:04:37,252 --> 00:04:38,837
and it snips them into maltose,

131
00:04:38,857 --> 00:04:41,127
over and over, without being used up.

132
00:04:41,147 --> 00:04:44,597
Your mouth is about 37
degrees and close to neutral:

133
00:04:44,617 --> 00:04:45,427
just right.

134
00:04:45,707 --> 00:04:48,917
Links that would last millions of
years on their own are broken while you

135
00:04:48,937 --> 00:04:51,907
chew. That sweetness
is enzymes at work,

136
00:04:51,927 --> 00:04:53,507
in real time.

137
00:04:53,527 --> 00:04:55,462
And that's how enzymes actually work.
